steps.body:
An end-of-day report needs the day's fills, which the trading system wrote to a file as they happened. `<fstream>` gives you two streams that work like the ones you know. A `std::ofstream` writes a file with `<<`, and a `std::ifstream` reads one with `>>` and `std::getline`:

```cpp
{
    std::ofstream out("fills.csv");  // creates the file, or empties an old one
    out << "time,symbol,qty\n";
    out << "09:30:01,AAPL,100\n";
}  // out's destructor flushes and closes the file
std::ifstream in("fills.csv");
std::string line;
while (std::getline(in, line)) {
    std::cout << line << '\n';
}
```

Opening a file is a system call. The kernel finds the path, checks your permissions, and hands the process a **file descriptor**, the small number that *Processes and System Calls*, the last lesson, met as 0, 1, and 2. A new one gets the lowest free number, 3 for a program's first file on a Mac, and 9 in the sandbox, which keeps a few of its own open. The stream keeps that number and a buffer, and its destructor closes the file, which is RAII from *Constructors, Destructors, and RAII*. To add to the end of a file instead of emptying it, open it with `std::ofstream out("fills.csv", std::ios::app)`.

Opening fails when the file is missing, its folder doesn't exist, or you lack permission. The stream doesn't stop the program. It sets its fail state, and every later read quietly gets nothing, so check right after opening:

```cpp
std::ifstream in("fills-2024-03-01.csv");
if (!in) {
    std::cout << "cannot open fills-2024-03-01.csv\n";
}
```

`in.is_open()` asks the same question. A CSV line splits the way *Using Input with Strings, Maps, Sets, and Structs* showed: `std::getline` reads the line, a `std::istringstream` wraps it, and `std::getline` with a `','` delimiter takes each field. Read the header line once before the loop, so it isn't parsed as data.

example.code:
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    {
        std::ofstream out("fills.csv");  // creates the file, or empties an old one
        out << "time,symbol,side,qty\n";
        out << "09:30:01,AAPL,B,100\n";
        out << "09:30:02,MSFT,S,50\n";
        out << "09:31:15,AAPL,S,30\n";
        out << "09:32:40,MSFT,S,25\n";
    }  // out is destroyed here: it flushes and closes the file

    std::ifstream in("fills.csv");
    if (!in) {
        std::cout << "cannot open fills.csv\n";
        return 1;
    }
    std::string line;
    std::getline(in, line);  // skip the header
    std::map<std::string, int> position;
    int fills = 0;
    while (std::getline(in, line)) {
        std::istringstream fields(line);
        std::string time, symbol, side, qty;
        std::getline(fields, time, ',');
        std::getline(fields, symbol, ',');
        std::getline(fields, side, ',');
        std::getline(fields, qty, ',');
        const int shares = std::stoi(qty);
        position[symbol] += (side == "B" ? shares : -shares);
        ++fills;
    }
    std::cout << fills << " fills read\n";
    for (const auto& [symbol, shares] : position) {
        std::cout << symbol << ' ' << shares << '\n';
    }

    std::ifstream old("fills-2024-03-01.csv");
    std::cout << (old ? "opened" : "cannot open") << " fills-2024-03-01.csv\n";
    return 0;
}

