steps.body:
A tick store keeps one file per trading day, like `data/ticks/2024-03-04.csv`. Before a backtest loads a month of them, it has to find the days, skip stray files, and check sizes. `<filesystem>`, new in C++17, does that the same way on every system. Code usually shortens its namespace first, with `namespace fs = std::filesystem;`.

- `fs::path` holds a path, and `/` joins parts: `fs::path("data") / "ticks"`. For `2024-03-04.csv`, `.filename()` is the whole name, `.stem()` is `2024-03-04`, and `.extension()` is `.csv`.
- `fs::create_directories(p)` makes every missing folder along `p`, like `mkdir -p` in a terminal. A `std::ofstream` can't make folders, so a file in a missing one fails to open.
- `fs::exists(p)` asks whether something is there, and `fs::file_size(p)` gives its size in bytes as a `std::uintmax_t`.
- `fs::directory_iterator(p)` visits each entry in a folder once, in whatever order the file system keeps them. Collect the paths in a vector and sort it for a stable order.

Print a path with `.string()`, since `std::cout << p` puts quotes around it.

When one of these functions fails, it throws an exception, which *Exceptions and Error Handling* covers a few lessons on. Uncaught, an exception ends the program. Pass a `std::error_code` from `<system_error>` as the last argument instead, and the function stores the error there and returns a fallback value. The code converts to `true` when it holds an error, and `.message()` gives the operating system's own words for it:

```cpp
std::error_code ec;
const std::uintmax_t size = std::filesystem::file_size("2024-03-07.csv", ec);
if (ec) {
    std::cout << "error: " << ec.message() << '\n';  // error: No such file or directory
} else {
    std::cout << size << " bytes\n";
}
```

g++ 10.2 needs no extra flag for `<filesystem>`, and neither do Apple clang and MSVC, though g++ 8 needed `-lstdc++fs`. On Windows a path may use `\` or `/`.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstdint>
#include <filesystem>
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <system_error>
#include <vector>

namespace fs = std::filesystem;

void write_file(const fs::path& p, const std::string& text) {
    std::ofstream out(p);
    out << text;
}

int main() {
    const fs::path dir = fs::path("data") / "ticks";
    fs::create_directories(dir);  // makes data, then data/ticks
    write_file(dir / "2024-03-05.csv", "time,price\n09:30:00,192.10\n09:30:01,192.15\n");
    write_file(dir / "2024-03-04.csv", "time,price\n09:30:00,191.80\n");
    write_file(dir / "2024-03-06.csv", "time,price\n09:30:00,193.00\n09:30:02,193.05\n09:30:04,192.95\n");
    write_file(dir / "README.txt", "one CSV per trading day\n");

    std::vector<fs::path> files;
    for (const fs::directory_entry& entry : fs::directory_iterator(dir)) {
        files.push_back(entry.path());
    }
    std::sort(files.begin(), files.end());  // the folder's own order isn't sorted

    std::uintmax_t total = 0;
    int days = 0;
    for (const fs::path& p : files) {
        if (p.extension() != ".csv") {
            std::cout << "skipped " << p.filename().string() << '\n';
            continue;
        }
        const std::uintmax_t size = fs::file_size(p);
        std::cout << p.stem().string() << ": " << size << " bytes\n";
        total += size;
        ++days;
    }
    std::cout << days << " days, " << total << " bytes\n";

    std::error_code ec;
    const fs::path missing = dir / "2024-03-07.csv";
    const std::uintmax_t size = fs::file_size(missing, ec);
    if (ec) {
        std::cout << missing.filename().string() << ": " << ec.message() << '\n';
    } else {
        std::cout << missing.filename().string() << ": " << size << " bytes\n";
    }
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << "exists: " << fs::exists(missing) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

