steps.body:
A busy symbol can trade 10^7 times a day. As text, a price like `19275` is five characters to format on the way out and parse on the way back. In **binary** it's the 8 bytes of an `int64_t`, copied out and back as they are. Open the file with `std::ios::binary`, which stops Windows from turning each `'\n'` byte into `"\r\n"` (Linux and macOS never change bytes), and move raw bytes with `write` and `read`:

```cpp
const int64_t price = 19275;
std::ofstream out("prices.bin", std::ios::binary);
out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&price), sizeof price);
out.close();
std::cout << std::filesystem::file_size("prices.bin") << " bytes\n";  // 8 bytes
```

`write` takes a pointer to the first byte and a count, and `reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&price)` treats the address of `price` as the start of its bytes, which *Conversions and Casts* covers fully later in the course. This is for plain values: integers, doubles, and structs of them. A `std::string`'s bytes hold a pointer to its heap buffer, so writing them saves an address, not text. The bytes keep the machine's own order, little-endian on x86-64 and ARM64 alike.

On the way to the disk your bytes pass two buffers:

1. The stream's own buffer, inside your process. It works like `std::cout`'s from *Processes and System Calls*: `write` and `<<` only copy into it, and when it fills, or on `flush()` or close, the stream makes one `write` system call for all of it.
2. The kernel's **page cache**, RAM that holds pages of files. The system call copies your bytes there and returns, and every program reading the file sees them at once. Linux writes changed pages to the disk within about 30 seconds.

So after `flush()`, a crash of your program loses nothing, but a power cut still can. A trade log that must survive one calls `fsync` on the file descriptor, which waits milliseconds for the disk (`FlushFileBuffers` on Windows, `fcntl(fd, F_FULLFSYNC)` for a true flush on a Mac). `std::endl` costs a system call per line here too: writing 10^5 lines to a file took about 26 ms with `std::endl` and 5 ms with `'\n'`, rough times from the emulated sandbox.

example.code:
#include <cstdint>
#include <filesystem>
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>

namespace fs = std::filesystem;

struct Trade {
    int64_t ts_ns;        // nanoseconds since midnight
    int64_t price_ticks;  // price in cents
    int64_t qty;
};

int main() {
    std::ofstream out("trades.bin", std::ios::binary);
    for (int64_t i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {
        const Trade t{34200000000000 + i * 1000000, 19275 + i % 7, 100 + i % 3};
        out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&t), sizeof t);
        if (i + 1 == 3 || i + 1 == 1000) {
            std::cout << i + 1 << " trades written, file size " << fs::file_size("trades.bin") << '\n';
        }
    }
    out.flush();  // one write system call for whatever the buffer holds
    std::cout << "after flush, file size " << fs::file_size("trades.bin") << '\n';
    out.close();

    std::ifstream in("trades.bin", std::ios::binary);
    Trade t{};
    int64_t count = 0;
    int64_t volume = 0;
    int64_t last_price = 0;
    while (in.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(&t), sizeof t)) {  // false once no whole Trade is left
        ++count;
        volume += t.qty;
        last_price = t.price_ticks;
    }
    std::cout << count << " trades read, volume " << volume << ", last price " << last_price << '\n';
    return 0;
}

