steps.body:
A backtest rereads the same tick file on every run, maybe a hundred times while you tune a strategy. Reading through a stream copies each byte twice, from the page cache into the stream's buffer and then into your variables, with a system call for every buffer. **Memory mapping** skips both. `mmap`, from `<sys/mman.h>` on Linux and macOS, asks the kernel to make the file's pages part of your address space. The pointer it returns reads the page cache's own pages, with no copies and no more system calls:

```cpp harness
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    {
        std::ofstream out("prices.bin", std::ios::binary);
        const int64_t prices[] = {19275, 19280, 19268};
        out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(prices), sizeof prices);
    }
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
const int fd = open("prices.bin", O_RDONLY);  // <fcntl.h>: a file descriptor
struct stat info{};                           // "struct", since stat is also a function
fstat(fd, &info);                             // <sys/stat.h>: st_size is the size
void* p = mmap(nullptr, info.st_size, PROT_READ, MAP_PRIVATE, fd, 0);
close(fd);                                    // the mapping stays valid
if (p != MAP_FAILED) {
    int64_t second = 0;
    std::memcpy(&second, static_cast<const char*>(p) + 8, sizeof second);
    std::cout << info.st_size << " bytes, second price " << second << '\n';  // 24 bytes, second price 19280
    munmap(p, info.st_size);
}
```

Nothing is read when `mmap` returns. The first touch of each page is a page fault, as in *Memory Hierarchy: Registers, Caches, RAM, Pages, and Disk*: the kernel maps the page in, reading it from the disk only if the page cache doesn't hold it yet, and reads ahead as you walk forward. On the next run the pages are still cached, so the file reads at memory speed, and processes that map one file share one copy of it in RAM.

Check the result against `MAP_FAILED`, not `nullptr`. Copy each value out with `std::memcpy(&dst, src, n)` from `<cstring>`, which copies `n` bytes and compiles to one load at `-O2`. Casting the pointer to `const int64_t*` looks the same, but it breaks a rule that *Binary Formats: Bit-Fields, Unions, and Endianness* explains a few lessons on.

Mapping pays for big files read many times, by many processes, or out of order. It doesn't for a small file, which one `read` handles, and a mapping can't grow with a file that's still being written. If another program shortens the file, touching the lost pages raises `SIGBUS`, a signal that ends the program. Windows maps files with `CreateFileMapping` and `MapViewOfFile`.

example.code:
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <sys/resource.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>

long page_faults() {
    rusage usage{};
    getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF, &usage);
    return usage.ru_minflt;
}

int64_t sum_prices(const char* bytes, std::size_t size) {
    int64_t sum = 0;
    for (std::size_t offset = 0; offset + 8 <= size; offset += 8) {
        int64_t price;
        std::memcpy(&price, bytes + offset, sizeof price);  // 8 bytes into an int64_t
        sum += price;
    }
    return sum;
}

int main() {
    {
        std::ofstream out("prices.bin", std::ios::binary);
        for (int64_t i = 0; i < 100000; ++i) {
            const int64_t price = 19270 + i % 11;
            out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&price), sizeof price);
        }
    }

    const int fd = open("prices.bin", O_RDONLY);
    struct stat info{};
    fstat(fd, &info);
    const std::size_t size = info.st_size;
    void* mapped = mmap(nullptr, size, PROT_READ, MAP_PRIVATE, fd, 0);
    close(fd);  // the mapping keeps the file open
    if (mapped == MAP_FAILED) {
        std::cout << "mmap failed\n";
        return 1;
    }
    const char* bytes = static_cast<const char*>(mapped);
    std::cout << size << " bytes mapped\n";

    long faults = page_faults();
    const int64_t first = sum_prices(bytes, size);
    faults = page_faults() - faults;
    std::cout << "first pass: sum " << first << ", page faults: " << (faults > 0 ? "some" : "none") << '\n';

    faults = page_faults();
    const int64_t second = sum_prices(bytes, size);
    faults = page_faults() - faults;
    std::cout << "second pass: sum " << second << ", page faults: " << (faults > 0 ? "some" : "none") << '\n';

    munmap(mapped, size);
    return 0;
}

